            <div xml:id="d18">

                <pb facs="0108" n="18" sameAs="frus:frus1934v03/pg18"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">894.00/505</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 656</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Tokyo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-01-26">January 26, 1934</date>.<lb/>[Received February
                        10.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: On passing through Tokyo today en route
                    from his post at Seoul to Vancouver, Consul General Davis told me that in his
                    farewell interview with Lieutenant General Kawashima, in command of the troops
                    in Chosen, the General spoke with enthusiasm of the great progress and
                    development in the arts of civilization which had taken place in Japan and added
                    his hope that the oppressed and suffering people of Manchuria, China and Siberia
                    would soon be enabled to share these Heaven-sent gifts. The foregoing is not a
                    precise quotation but conveys the sense of the General’s remarks as repeated to
                    me by Mr. Davis. They represent, perhaps, the thoughts of a certain type of
                    Japanese military mind.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Joseph C. Grew</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
